EXPEDITED REVIEW:

This plat is being pursued as an expedited application.  An expedited review process is a
concurrent preliminary and final plat. A project may be eligible for an expedited review when the
proposal contains no significant planning, design and engineering issues.  In other words
proposals that meet all applicable regulations and generally meet the review criteria for
preliminary and final plat may be eligible for the concurrent preliminary and final plat process.

Staff recommends allowing the plat to proceed as an expedited application.

EXECUTIVE SUMMARY:

The expedited preliminary plat before you is for approximately 13,365 square feet of land located
at the northeast corner of the intersection of Randall and Snyder Avenues. This application was
submitted in conjunction with a zone change application to provide consistent zoning across the
entire subject site to NB Neighborhood Business. The purpose of this plat application is to
combine three platted lots into a single lot. The NB Neighborhood Business zone district and the
district regulations are designed to encourage uses like convenience stores, while also protecting
the abutting and nearby residential areas. This application is in compliance with the
comprehensive plan, reflects sound planning principles, and is not anticipated to have any undue
influence on present or future development of adjacent parcels.

Due to meeting the review criteria found in Section 2.1.2.d of the Cheyenne Unified
Development Code (UDC), staff recommends approval of this expedited preliminary
plat, advancing the final plat to the Governing Body.
```

### Page 6 of 10

```text
Expedited Preliminary Plat/UDC-15-00411
4
PLAT DATA:  In reviewing, providing comments, and making recommendations and decisions on a
proposed preliminary plat, the Staff, Planning Commission and City Council shall use the following
criteria:

PLANCHEYENNE - The application is in accordance with the development policies and physical patterns
identified in the PlanCheyenne.

The future land use map, which is described in PlanCheyenne, has designated this area as Urban
Residential. Urban Residential areas are intended for a variety of residential types, including
single-family residences, duplexes, patio homes, townhomes, condominiums, and apartments.
Secondary uses include open spaces and recreation, schools, places of worship, and other public
or civic uses are encouraged. The NB zone district can work within the Urban Residential area.
NB has more restrictions on the uses allowed and is intended to service the convenience
shopping needs of a neighborhood. They are characterized by a compact scale and pedestrian-
friendly design that provides ease of access from adjacent neighborhoods.

PLANNING - The application reflects sound planning and urban design principles with respect to existing
and potential future development in the vicinity, including consideration of current and future streets,
open spaces, blocks and lots, and public utilities or facilities.

The application is in conformance with the design principles of the zoning code and the
subdivision regulations and further, is consistent with PlanCheyenne as noted above. This
application proposes to create a single lot and block for an existing commercial use immediately
adjacent to Randall and Snyder Avenues, ultimately creating a site that is more suited to
redevelopment, should that opportunity ever arise.

STANDARDS - Division of land meets the purposes, intent, and all applicable standards in Article 4.

Transportation Network:
There are not any new rights-of-way proposed with plat.

Community Facility Fees:
Park Fees:
No fees will be assessed as the application meets one of the exemptions under 4.2.5.c.

Open Space:
Open Space was evaluated at the time of the Site Plan according to the standards set forth by
the Cheyenne UDC.

PHASING - Any phasing proposed in the application is clearly indicated and demonstrates a logical and
well-planned development pattern. This may include consideration of amenities and public facilities of the
subdivision within each phase compared to the anticipated timing or the likelihood of future phases being
constructed as planned.

There are no proposed phases for this plat.
